{
"dur": 50.835,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.519,
"text": "In 1964, Knight and Bowerman put in 500 dollars each. Blue Ribbon Sports was real.",
"end": 7.794
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.794,
"vo_start": 8.069,
"vo_end": 12.666,
"text": "Knight sold the shoes from the trunk of his car at track meets across the Pacific Northwest.",
"end": 12.941
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 12.941,
"vo_start": 13.216,
"vo_end": 19.896,
"text": "First year: about 1,300 pairs, roughly 8,000 dollars. He kept his day job as an accountant.",
"end": 20.171
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 20.171,
"vo_start": 20.446,
"vo_end": 28.286,
"text": "His first full-time employee, Jeff Johnson, wrote personal letters to customers, noted their race times and even their birthdays, and opened the first store in Santa Monica.",
"end": 28.561
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 28.561,
"vo_start": 28.836,
"vo_end": 37.906,
"text": "Meanwhile Bowerman wrote a little book called Jogging, which helped set off the American running boom. The coach wasn't just making shoes. He was making customers.",
"end": 38.181
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 38.181,
"vo_start": 38.456,
"vo_end": 46.856,
"text": "Then, in 1971, came the waffle iron. Ruined, as we saw. But its grid of squares became the waffle sole: grip without weight.",
"end": 47.131
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 47.131,
"vo_start": 47.406,
"vo_end": 49.885,
"text": "The Bowermans had to buy a new waffle iron.",
"end": 50.835
}
],
